export const H6={title:'Butterfly venipuncture and special populations (Skill III-B)',hook:'An 88-year-old patient has thin skin and veins that roll under your finger. A 6-year-old is frightened and crying. A patient’s antecubital veins are scarred from years of draws. The winged infusion set, or butterfly, gives you control and a gentle angle for small and fragile veins. Skill III-B has 40 steps in 10 minutes.',objectives:['Describe when to use a butterfly and how it differs from a straight needle.','Perform a butterfly venipuncture with an evacuated tube adapter following HOSA Skill III-B.','Explain the discard tube and other technical points for butterflies.','Adapt technique for children, older adults and patients with difficult veins.'],sections:[
['When to use a butterfly',[
'A **winged infusion set (butterfly)** has a short needle (often **23 gauge**, sometimes 21 or 25) attached to plastic wings and **flexible tubing** that connects to a **tube holder (evacuated tube adapter)** or syringe. It is used for **small, fragile or rolling veins**, **hand veins**, **older adults**, **children**, patients with **burns or scarring** in the antecubital area and patients who are difficult to draw. The flexible tubing lets you hold the needle steady even when you change tubes.',
'Because the tubing contains air, the first tube drawn will be **underfilled**. If the first tube is a **light-blue coagulation tube**, draw a **discard tube** (a plain red or another light-blue tube) first to fill the tubing with blood so the citrate tube fills completely. Butterflies cost more and are associated with more needlestick injuries if handled carelessly, so activate the safety feature immediately and do not let the needle dangle.',
sort('Straight ETS needle or butterfly?',['Routine straight needle','Butterfly is helpful'],[['Healthy adult with a large median cubital vein',0],['Older adult with fragile hand veins',1],['Young child',1],['Patient with scarred antecubital veins',1],['Routine draw on a cooperative adult with good veins',0]])]],
['Preparing for Skill III-B: steps 1–16',[
'The first steps match Skill III-A: **1.** review the requisition; **2.** greet the patient and identify yourself; **3.** ask the patient to state their full name and date of birth and spell their names; **4.** match them to the requisition; **5.** explain and obtain consent; **6.** verify requirements and sensitivities; **7.** hand hygiene and gloves; **8.** position the arm or hand; **9.** apply the tourniquet 3–4 inches above the site (for a hand draw, just above the wrist bone); **10.** ask the patient to **close the hand**.',
'**11.** Palpate and select a vein in the **appropriate area**. **12.** Release the tourniquet and ask the patient to relax the hand. **13.** Note the vein’s location relative to hair, creases, scars or moles. **14.** Clean with back-and-forth friction. **15.** Air-dry. **16.** Prepare equipment by **attaching the butterfly to an evacuated tube adapter** (holder), and stretch the coiled tubing slightly so it does not spring back.',
order('Hand draw setup',['Position the hand with fingers slightly bent over a rolled towel','Tourniquet just above the wrist','Patient closes the hand; palpate','Release; clean and air-dry','Attach the butterfly to the tube adapter','Reapply the tourniquet'])]],
['Inserting and filling: steps 17–29',[
'**17.** Reapply the tourniquet. **18.** Uncap and inspect the needle. **19.** Ask the patient to close the hand. **20.** **Anchor the vein below the site**. **21.** Hold the butterfly with the **wings upward** (pinched together) and **bevel up**. **22.** Insert the needle smoothly at the **correct (shallow) angle**—about **10–15 degrees** for small hand veins—and watch for a **“flash”** of blood in the tubing. **23.** Maintain placement by holding the wings or resting them flat against the skin.',
'**24.** Grasp the short wings of the tube holder with the index and middle fingers. **25.** Push the tube into the needle with the thumb. **26.** Let the tube fill until the vacuum is exhausted. **27.** Complete the draw in the **order of draw** (light blue; red/gold; green; lavender/pink/pearl). **28.** Fill, remove and mix tubes per manufacturer instructions. Use **smaller-volume tubes** for small veins—a large vacuum can **collapse** the vein or cause **hemolysis** through a small needle. **29.** Remove the tourniquet when the last tube starts to fill.',
chk('Why use smaller-volume tubes with a 23-gauge butterfly?',['A strong vacuum can collapse small veins or hemolyze the sample','They are cheaper','They clot faster','They do not need labels'],'Match tube size to vein size.')]],
['Finishing: steps 30–40',[
'**30.** Remove the needle smoothly and **activate the safety feature**. **31.** Apply folded gauze and pressure; mix tubes per manufacturer instructions. **32.** Ask the patient to maintain pressure until bleeding stops. **33.** Check for bleeding and bandage. **34.** Discard the entire butterfly unit—needle, tubing and holder—in the **sharps container**. **35.** Label and bag the tubes.',
'**36.** Dispose of materials and clean the workspace. **37.** Thank the patient. **38.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. **39–40.** Communicate appropriately and use standard precautions throughout. Coil the tubing into your hand or let the safety device lock before moving toward the sharps container, because loose tubing can swing the needle back toward you.',
flip('Butterfly reminders',[['Gauge','Usually 23 G'],['Angle','Shallow, about 10–15° for hand veins'],['Flash','Blood appears in the tubing when you enter the vein'],['Discard tube','Draw first if a light-blue tube is first'],['Tubes','Use smaller-volume tubes for small veins'],['Disposal','Entire unit into the sharps container']])]],
['Children and older adults',[
'**Children**: involve the parent or guardian, explain in simple words, be honest that it will pinch, use distraction (counting, bubbles, videos) and have another trained person help hold the child still—never restrain without assistance and policy. Infants under one year usually have capillary or heel punctures instead of venipuncture. Collect the **minimum blood volume** needed, because small bodies have small blood volumes.',
'**Older adults** may have thin skin, fragile veins, reduced circulation, arthritis that limits arm extension, hearing or vision loss and anticoagulant use. Speak clearly, allow time, apply the tourniquet loosely or over a sleeve, anchor gently, use a shallow angle and smaller tubes, hold pressure longer and use paper tape or a gentle bandage to avoid tearing skin. Patients with **dementia** may move suddenly, so ask a helper to support the arm.',
match('Adapt the approach',[['Toddler','Parent present, distraction, a helper to keep the arm still'],['Infant under 1 year','Usually heel puncture'],['Older adult with thin skin','Gentle tape and longer pressure'],['Patient on anticoagulants','Hold pressure longer'],['Patient with dementia','Simple words and a helper'],['Patient with hearing loss','Face them and speak clearly']])]],
['Difficult draws',[
'For difficult veins, **warm** the site with a warm pack for a few minutes to increase blood flow, **lower the arm** below heart level, **gently massage** from wrist toward elbow and palpate both arms before choosing. Do not slap the arm, which can cause petechiae and hemolysis. If the first attempt fails, you may adjust once; after **two unsuccessful attempts**, ask another qualified phlebotomist. Never probe or move the needle sideways searching for a vein, which can injure nerves.',
kase('Fragile vein draw',{a:{text:'An older patient’s antecubital veins are not palpable, but you find a hand vein. What equipment do you choose?',choices:[['A 23-gauge butterfly with a tube adapter and small-volume tubes','b','Correct. Gentle equipment for a small vein.',true],['A 21-gauge straight needle with large tubes','b','Large vacuum tubes can collapse small veins.',false]]},b:{text:'The requisition lists only a PT/INR in a light-blue tube. What must you draw first?',choices:[['A discard tube to fill the butterfly tubing','c','Correct. Otherwise the light-blue tube underfills.',true],['Nothing—start with the light blue','c','The tubing’s air would underfill the citrate tube.',false]]},c:{text:'You collect a full light-blue tube, hold pressure longer and apply paper tape. Gentle and accurate.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Butterflies (usually 23 G) suit small, fragile, hand and pediatric veins; the tubing holds air, so draw a discard tube if light blue is first.','Skill III-B: same identification and preparation as III-A; attach the butterfly to the tube adapter; wings up, bevel up, shallow angle, watch for the flash, hold placement steady and fill in the order of draw.','Use small tubes, remove the tourniquet as the last tube fills, activate the safety device, discard the whole unit; adapt for children and older adults; no more than two attempts.'],terms:[['Winged infusion set','A butterfly needle with wings and flexible tubing.'],['Discard tube','A tube drawn first to clear air from butterfly tubing.'],['Flash','The appearance of blood in the tubing or hub when the needle enters the vein.']],mcqs:[q('What gauge is most common for butterfly needles in phlebotomy?','23 gauge','16 gauge','18 gauge','30 gauge','Small and fragile veins.'),q('Why is a discard tube drawn before a light-blue tube with a butterfly?','Air in the tubing would underfill the citrate tube','To warm the vein','To test the needle','It is never needed','The 9:1 ratio requires a full tube.'),q('How should the butterfly be held for insertion?','Wings upward and bevel up','Wings flat and bevel down','By the tubing','By the tube holder only','Per Skill III-B.'),q('What angle is typical for a hand vein with a butterfly?','About 10–15 degrees','45 degrees','90 degrees','30–45 degrees','Shallow angle.'),q('Why use smaller-volume tubes with small veins?','Large vacuums can collapse the vein','They hold more blood','They are faster','They do not need to be inverted','Also reduces hemolysis.'),q('What is discarded in the sharps container after a butterfly draw?','The entire unit including tubing and holder','Only the needle','Only the tubing','Nothing','Discard together.'),q('How many unsuccessful attempts before asking another phlebotomist?','Two','One','Five','Unlimited','Common policy.'),q('Which technique increases blood flow to a difficult vein?','Warming the site','Slapping the arm','Leaving the tourniquet on 3 minutes','Vigorous fist pumping','Gentle warming is safe.')],frq:{prompt:'An older adult with fragile hand veins needs a PT/INR and a CBC. Explain how you would perform the collection using a butterfly following Skill III-B, including equipment choices and order of draw.',rubric:[['Equipment',['butterfly','23','tube adapter','small','discard']],['Technique',['tourniquet','wrist','anchor','wings','bevel up','shallow','flash','steady']],['Order and tubes',['discard','light blue','lavender','invert','fill']],['Finishing and care',['tourniquet','safety','pressure','longer','tape','sharps','label']]],model:'After identifying the patient by having them state and spell their name and date of birth, matching the requisition, explaining and obtaining consent and checking for anticoagulant use, I perform hand hygiene and put on gloves. I choose a 23-gauge butterfly attached to an evacuated tube adapter and small-volume tubes so the vacuum does not collapse the fragile vein. I support the hand over a rolled towel, apply the tourniquet just above the wrist, palpate the vein, release the tourniquet, clean with friction and let it air-dry, then reapply the tourniquet and inspect the needle. I anchor the vein below the site, hold the butterfly with the wings up and bevel up and insert it at a shallow angle of about 10–15 degrees, watching for the flash, and keep it steady by holding the wings. Because the PT/INR light-blue tube comes first, I draw a discard tube to clear air from the tubing, then fill the light-blue tube completely and the lavender tube for the CBC, inverting each gently. I release the tourniquet as the last tube fills, withdraw the needle and activate the safety device, apply pressure longer than usual, use gentle paper tape, discard the whole unit in the sharps container and label the tubes at the bedside.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_BLOOD,MEDLINE_VENI]};

export const H7={title:'Complications: fainting, arterial sticks and other problems (Skills IV and VII)',hook:'Most venipunctures go smoothly, but phlebotomists must be ready when they do not. A patient suddenly turns pale and sweaty. Bright red blood pulses into the tube. A hematoma swells under the needle. Knowing the right steps in the right order protects patients and specimens. Skills IV and VII test two of the most important responses.',objectives:['Recognize the signs of syncope and respond following HOSA Skill IV.','Recognize an accidental arterial stick and respond following HOSA Skill VII.','Prevent and manage hematomas, nerve injury, petechiae and other complications.','Identify preanalytical errors such as hemolysis and hemoconcentration.'],sections:[
['Recognizing fainting',[
'**Syncope** (fainting) is a brief loss of consciousness, often caused by a sudden drop in blood pressure triggered by anxiety, pain, the sight of blood, fasting or standing too long (a **vasovagal** reaction). Warning signs include **pallor**, **heavy perspiration**, **shallow or rapid breathing**, dizziness, nausea, a feeling of warmth and a weak, slow pulse. Asking about previous fainting and having patients **sit in a phlebotomy chair with arms**, or **lie down** if they have fainted before, prevents injuries.',
'In Skill IV the judge says: “Heavy perspiration, pale skin, and shallow or fast breathing are noted.” Your priority is to **stop the procedure safely** and **prevent a fall**. Never leave a patient who feels faint, and do not let them get up or drive until they have fully recovered and been cleared.',
sort('Possible signs of fainting?',['Warning sign','Not a warning sign'],[['Pale skin',0],['Heavy sweating',0],['Shallow, rapid breathing',0],['Talking and joking normally',1],['Dizziness',0],['Steady pink color',1]])]],
['Responding to fainting: Skill IV',[
'Skill IV has 17 steps in 5 minutes. After the judge describes the signs: **2.** **Immediately release the tourniquet**. **3.** **Remove the needle**, activate the safety device and discard it. **4.** **Call for help**. **5.** **Apply pressure** to the site. **6.** **Stand in front of** the phlebotomy chair (or next to the bed) to keep the patient from falling or slipping. **7.** **Lower the patient’s head and arms** by placing both **between the patient’s knees** (in the chair). **8.** Instruct the patient to **breathe deeply**.',
'**9.** **Loosen tight clothing**. **10.** **Verbalize that no ammonia inhalant was used**—ammonia can cause airway spasm or sudden movement. **11.** Apply a **cold compress** or wet washcloth to the forehead and back of the neck if available. **12.** Provide a **basin** or trash can in case of nausea and vomiting. **13.** **Stay with the patient** until appropriate personnel arrive (the judge announces that the outpatient supervisor or charge nurse has arrived). **14.** **Document** the incident on the chart. **15.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. **16–17.** Communicate appropriately and use standard precautions throughout.',
order('Skill IV sequence',['Release the tourniquet','Remove the needle and activate the safety device','Call for help','Apply pressure to the site','Stand in front of the patient to prevent a fall','Lower head and arms between the knees','Breathe deeply; loosen tight clothing','No ammonia; cold compress; basin if nauseated','Stay until help arrives; document'])]],
['Accidental arterial stick: Skill VII',[
'An **accidental arterial stick** happens most often when drawing the **basilic vein**, which lies near the **brachial artery**. **1.** Verbalize the indications: a **rapidly forming hematoma** and **bright red blood pulsing** into the tube. **2.** **Release the tourniquet**. **3.** **Remove the needle**, activate the safety device and discard it in the sharps container. **4.** Apply **firm pressure for at least 5 minutes**, or until bleeding stops—arterial pressure is high. **5.** When bleeding stops, apply a **taut gauze dressing**.',
'**6.** Instruct the patient to keep the arm relatively still for a short time. **7.** **Inform the charge nurse** (judge). **8.** Verbalize **checking with the laboratory supervisor** whether the blood is usable—arterial values differ for some tests. **9.** If acceptable, label the tube with the patient’s **name and date of birth and “arterial blood.”** **10.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. **11.** **Document** the incident. **12–13.** Communicate appropriately and use standard precautions.',
order('Skill VII sequence',['Recognize bright red pulsing blood and a fast hematoma','Release the tourniquet','Remove the needle and activate the safety device','Firm pressure for at least 5 minutes','Taut gauze dressing when bleeding stops','Inform the charge nurse','Ask the lab supervisor if the blood is usable','Label “arterial blood” with name and DOB; document'])]],
['Other complications',[
'**Hematomas** form when blood leaks into tissue. Prevent them by puncturing only the upper wall of the vein, anchoring well, removing the tourniquet before the needle, using enough pressure and keeping the arm straight. If one starts forming, stop, remove the needle and apply pressure. **Nerve injury** causes sharp, shooting or electric pain, tingling or numbness; **remove the needle immediately**, apply pressure, document and report. Avoid deep or lateral probing and the basilic area when possible.',
'**Petechiae** are tiny red spots that can appear from a tight tourniquet or a platelet problem; they warn you that the site may bleed longer. **Excessive bleeding** occurs with anticoagulants or bleeding disorders—hold pressure until it stops and do not leave the patient. **Allergies** to latex, adhesive or antiseptics require alternatives. **Seizures** require stopping the draw, protecting the patient from injury and calling for help. Patients who have **vomited** or are **nauseated** should be given a basin and allowed to rest.',
"Some patients have **needle phobia** or severe anxiety. Ask them to lie down, explain each step, use distraction and allow them to look away. Never rush or trick a patient. If a patient becomes combative or refuses, stop and notify the nurse. After any complication, document what happened, what you did and who you notified—accurate records protect both the patient and the phlebotomist.",
match('Complication → response',[['Shooting, electric pain','Remove the needle immediately'],['Swelling at the site during the draw','Release tourniquet, remove needle, apply pressure'],['Bright red pulsing blood','Treat as an arterial stick'],['Tiny red spots below the tourniquet','Expect longer bleeding; hold pressure'],['Seizure during the draw','Stop, protect from injury, call for help'],['Latex allergy','Use non-latex gloves and tourniquet']])]],
['Preanalytical errors',[
'Most laboratory errors happen **before** testing, during collection and handling. **Hemolysis** falsely raises potassium, LDH and AST; causes include small needles with large tubes, shaking, wet alcohol and difficult draws. **Hemoconcentration** from long tourniquet time or fist pumping raises proteins, potassium and cells. **Wrong order of draw** causes additive carryover. **Underfilled** tubes alter ratios (especially light blue). **Wrong timing** ruins peak/trough and glucose tolerance tests.',
'**Patient factors** also matter: fasting status, posture (lying versus standing changes some values), exercise, stress, smoking, medications and diurnal variation (cortisol and iron are higher in the morning). **IV contamination** occurs when blood is drawn above an IV. Recognizing these errors and documenting conditions helps the laboratory interpret results.',
"Many laboratories track **quality indicators** such as hemolysis rates, recollection rates, mislabeled specimens and blood culture contamination rates (often targeted at **3% or less**). Phlebotomists who keep these rates low through careful technique directly improve patient care and reduce costs.",
sort('Which error?',['Hemolysis','Hemoconcentration','Additive carryover'],[['Shaking a lavender tube',0],['Tourniquet on for 3 minutes',1],['Drawing lavender before light blue',2],['Drawing through wet alcohol',0],['Vigorous fist pumping',1],['EDTA contaminating an SST',2]])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Fainting in the chair',{a:{text:'Mid-draw, the judge says the patient has heavy perspiration, pale skin and fast, shallow breathing. What is your first action?',choices:[['Release the tourniquet immediately','b','Correct. Then remove the needle.',true],['Finish filling the tubes quickly','b','Patient safety comes first.',false]]},b:{text:'The needle is out and pressure applied. A coworker hands you an ammonia inhalant. What do you say?',choices:[['“No ammonia inhalant will be used.”','c','Correct. Verbalize that no ammonia is used.',true],['Wave it under the patient’s nose','c','Ammonia is not used in this skill.',false]]},c:{text:'You lower the patient’s head between the knees, loosen clothing, apply a cold compress, stay until the charge nurse arrives and document.',end:true}}),
chk('How long should pressure be held after an accidental arterial stick?',['At least 5 minutes or until bleeding stops','30 seconds','1 minute exactly','No pressure is needed'],'Arterial bleeding takes longer to stop.')]]
],takeaways:['Skill IV: release tourniquet, remove needle, call for help, pressure, stand in front, head and arms between knees, breathe deeply, loosen clothing, no ammonia, cold compress, basin, stay, document.','Skill VII: bright red pulsing blood and a fast hematoma; release tourniquet, remove needle, firm pressure at least 5 minutes, taut gauze, inform the nurse, ask the lab, label “arterial blood,” document.','Prevent hematomas and nerve injury with good technique; stop immediately for shooting pain; avoid hemolysis, hemoconcentration, carryover and underfilling.'],terms:[['Syncope','Fainting, a brief loss of consciousness.'],['Vasovagal reaction','A sudden drop in heart rate and blood pressure triggered by stress or pain.'],['Preanalytical error','An error that occurs before the specimen is tested.']],mcqs:[q('What is the first step when a patient shows signs of fainting during a draw?','Release the tourniquet','Call the patient’s family','Give water','Use an ammonia inhalant','Then remove the needle.'),q('Where should the phlebotomist stand during a fainting episode?','In front of the chair to prevent a fall','Behind the patient','In the hallway','At the sharps container','Prevent slipping.'),q('What should you verbalize about ammonia inhalants in Skill IV?','None was used','One was used','It is always used','It replaces calling for help','Per the rating sheet.'),q('Which signs suggest an accidental arterial stick?','Bright red pulsing blood and a rapidly forming hematoma','Dark red slow flow','No blood flow','Pale skin and sweating','Arterial pressure is high.'),q('After an arterial stick, how is the tube labeled if usable?','Name, DOB and “arterial blood”','“Venous blood”','Not labeled','With the nurse’s name only','The lab must know.'),q('Shooting, electric pain during the draw means:','Possible nerve contact—remove the needle immediately','The draw is going well','The tourniquet is loose','The tube is full','Avoid nerve injury.'),q('Which preanalytical error falsely raises potassium?','Hemolysis','Fasting','Proper inversion','Correct order of draw','Ruptured cells release potassium.'),q('Which vein is most often associated with accidental arterial sticks?','Basilic','Median cubital','Cephalic','Dorsal hand vein','It lies near the brachial artery.')],frq:{prompt:'During a venipuncture, the patient becomes pale and sweaty, and you also notice bright red blood pulsing into the tube. Explain how you would recognize and respond to both an impending faint and an accidental arterial stick, following Skills IV and VII.',rubric:[['Recognition',['pale','perspiration','shallow','bright red','pulsing','hematoma']],['Immediate actions',['release the tourniquet','remove the needle','safety','call for help','pressure','5 minutes']],['Fainting care',['stand in front','head','knees','breathe','loosen','no ammonia','cold compress','basin','stay']],['Follow-up',['taut gauze','charge nurse','laboratory supervisor','arterial blood','document']]],model:'Pale skin, heavy perspiration and shallow or fast breathing tell me the patient may faint, and bright red blood pulsing into the tube with a rapidly forming hematoma tells me I have accidentally punctured an artery. I immediately release the tourniquet, remove the needle, activate the safety device and discard it, and call for help. I apply firm pressure to the site for at least 5 minutes or until the bleeding stops, because arterial bleeding takes longer to control, and then apply a taut gauze dressing. At the same time I stand in front of the chair so the patient cannot fall, have them lower their head and arms between their knees, tell them to breathe deeply and loosen tight clothing. I do not use an ammonia inhalant, and I verbalize that. I apply a cold compress to the forehead and back of the neck, provide a basin if they feel nauseated and stay with them until the charge nurse or supervisor arrives. I inform the charge nurse of the arterial stick, ask the laboratory supervisor whether the specimen is usable and, if so, label it with the patient’s name and date of birth and “arterial blood.” Finally, I remove my gloves, perform hand hygiene and document both incidents.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_FAINT,WHO_BLOOD,AMT]};

export const H8={title:'Capillary and heel puncture (Skill V)',hook:'A newborn’s heel holds the key to screening for dozens of serious but treatable disorders, from phenylketonuria to sickle cell disease. Capillary puncture also serves adults who need a quick glucose check or whose veins cannot be used. The lancet must go deep enough to collect blood but no deeper than 2 mm in an infant’s heel. Skill V has 28 steps in 5 minutes.',objectives:['Explain when capillary puncture is used and how capillary blood differs from venous blood.','Select safe sites for heel and finger punctures and the correct depth.','State the order of draw for capillary specimens.','Perform a heel puncture following HOSA Skill V, including newborn screening cards.'],sections:[
['When to use capillary puncture',[
'**Capillary (skin) puncture** collects a small amount of blood from capillary beds. It is used for **infants and young children**, for **point-of-care tests** such as glucose and hemoglobin, for patients with **fragile or inaccessible veins**, **burns** or **severe obesity**, and when venous access must be preserved (for example, chemotherapy patients). Capillary blood is a mix of **arterial and venous blood plus tissue fluid**, so some results differ slightly from venous results; glucose is often higher.',
'Capillary puncture is **not** appropriate for blood cultures, most coagulation studies, large-volume tests or patients who are dehydrated or have poor circulation (cold, swollen or blue extremities). Equipment includes **safety lancets** with set depths, **microcollection containers** (“bullets”) with color-coded additives, **capillary tubes**, **newborn screening cards**, warming devices, alcohol pads and gauze.',
sort('Capillary puncture appropriate?',['Appropriate','Use venipuncture instead'],[['Newborn bilirubin',0],['Blood culture',1],['Fingerstick glucose',0],['Large panel needing several mL',1],['Hemoglobin screening in a clinic',0],['Patient with cold, swollen hands',1]])]],
['Sites and depth',[
'For **infants under 1 year**, use the **medial or lateral plantar surface of the heel**—the outer edges of the bottom of the heel—to avoid the **calcaneus** (heel bone). Puncturing the center of the heel or the back curve can cause **osteomyelitis** (bone infection). The puncture must be **no more than 2.0 mm deep**. Avoid previous puncture sites, bruised or swollen areas, and the arch.',
'For **older children and adults**, use the **palmar surface of the distal segment** of the **middle or ring finger** of the non-dominant hand, slightly off-center and **perpendicular to the fingerprint whorls** so the drop beads instead of running. Avoid the thumb (pulse), index finger (calloused, sensitive) and pinky (bone too close). Typical lancet depths for fingers are about **1.5–2.0 mm** depending on device and age.',
match('Site rules',[['Infant heel site','Medial or lateral plantar surface'],['Infant heel depth','No more than 2.0 mm'],['Bone to avoid in the heel','Calcaneus'],['Adult finger choice','Middle or ring finger'],['Finger to avoid (pulse)','Thumb'],['Lancet direction on the finger','Across the fingerprint whorls']])]],
['Order of draw for capillary specimens',[
'The capillary order of draw differs from the venous order because platelets clump quickly at the puncture site. CLSI recommends: **1.** blood gases (capillary gas tube), **2.** **EDTA** (lavender) so the hematology sample is collected before clotting begins, **3.** other **additive** tubes (such as heparin), **4.** **serum** (no additive) tubes last. Newborn screening cards are collected per the laboratory’s protocol, often first or separately from other tubes.',
'Microcollection containers are filled by **touching the scoop to the drop**, not scraping the skin, and gently tapped so blood flows down. Do not **milk** or squeeze hard—that adds tissue fluid and causes hemolysis. Gentle, intermittent pressure some distance from the site is acceptable. Cap and mix additive containers immediately.',
order('Capillary order of draw',['Blood gases','EDTA (lavender)','Other additive tubes (e.g., heparin)','Serum (non-additive) tubes'])]],
['Heel puncture: Skill V steps 1–15',[
'**1.** Review the test request. **2.** Approach, greet and **identify the patient using two identifiers**. **3.** Explain and obtain **consent from parents** if present. **4.** Verify collection requirements and sensitivities. **5.** Prepare equipment and supplies. **6.** Hand hygiene and gloves. **7.** Position the infant **face up with the foot lower than the torso**. **8.** Select the **medial or lateral plantar surface** of the heel, avoiding bruised or swollen areas and previous sites. **9.** Verbalize **warming the site for 3–5 minutes** with a warm wet washcloth, towel, diaper or commercial heel warmer (the judge will say it has been five minutes).',
'**10.** Clean with an **alcohol pad** using back-and-forth friction and **air-dry**. **11.** **Grasp the heel firmly** with the non-dominant hand, encircling the heel by wrapping your **index finger around the arch** and your **thumb around the bottom**, other fingers around the foot. **12.** Hold the lancet between your thumb and index finger. **13.** Place the lancet **flat against the skin** for good contact and activate it. **14.** The puncture is **no more than 2 mm** deep. **15.** Remove the lancet and **immediately discard** it in the sharps container.',
order('Skill V preparation and puncture',['Identify with two identifiers; consent from parents','Hand hygiene and gloves','Infant face up, foot lower than the torso','Select the medial or lateral plantar heel','Warm 3–5 minutes','Clean with alcohol and air-dry','Grasp the heel: index finger on the arch, thumb on the bottom','Lancet flat against the skin; activate (≤2 mm)','Discard the lancet immediately'])]],
['Collecting and finishing: steps 16–28',[
'**16.** Lower the heel and apply **gentle pressure** until a drop forms. **17.** Verbalize **wiping away the first drop** with gauze—it contains tissue fluid and alcohol residue. (The judge places a drop of simulated blood afterward.) **18.** Form a drop and **scoop** it into the collection tube for the requested test. **19.** Fill and mix tubes or containers in the **order of draw** for the scenario. For **newborn screening cards**, apply one large drop to fill each circle completely **from one side only**, without touching the card to the skin or layering drops.',
'**20.** Place gauze, **elevate the heel** and apply pressure until bleeding stops. **21.** Label and prepare the specimen. **22.** Check the site—**no bandage** is applied to infants because of the **choking risk**. **23.** Discard used materials. **24.** Thank the patient and parent or guardian. **25.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. **26.** **Document** on the chart. **27–28.** Communicate appropriately and use standard precautions throughout. Newborn screening cards **air-dry horizontally** for at least 3 hours away from heat and sunlight before being sent to the state laboratory.',
flip('Heel stick details',[['First drop','Wipe away with gauze'],['Collecting','Scoop the drop; do not scrape the skin'],['Milking','Avoid—causes hemolysis and tissue fluid'],['Screening card circles','Fill from one side, one drop per circle'],['After collection','Elevate the heel and apply pressure'],['Bandage','None for infants—choking risk']])]],
['Newborn screening and practice',[
'Every U.S. state screens newborns for a panel of genetic, metabolic, endocrine and hearing disorders, typically from a heel stick **24–48 hours after birth**. Early detection of conditions such as **phenylketonuria (PKU)**, **congenital hypothyroidism** and **sickle cell disease** allows treatment before serious harm. Poorly collected cards—**layered drops, unfilled circles, clots or contamination**—must be repeated, delaying results.',
kase('Heel puncture decisions',{a:{text:'You prepare to puncture the center of the back of an infant’s heel. Is that correct?',choices:[['No—use the medial or lateral plantar surface to avoid the calcaneus','b','Correct. Central punctures risk bone infection.',true],['Yes, the center gives the most blood','b','It risks hitting the calcaneus.',false]]},b:{text:'Blood flow is slow. A colleague suggests squeezing the heel hard. What do you do?',choices:[['Apply gentle intermittent pressure and keep the heel lower than the body','c','Correct. Hard squeezing causes hemolysis and dilutes the sample.',true],['Squeeze firmly and continuously','c','Milking alters results.',false]]},c:{text:'You collect the specimen, elevate the heel, apply pressure, skip the bandage and document. Well done.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Capillary puncture suits infants, point-of-care tests and difficult veins, but not blood cultures or large volumes; capillary blood mixes arterial, venous and tissue fluid.','Infants: medial or lateral plantar heel, no deeper than 2 mm, avoiding the calcaneus; adults: middle or ring finger, across the whorls; capillary order: gases, EDTA, other additives, serum.','Skill V: two identifiers, parental consent, foot lower than torso, warm 3–5 minutes, clean and dry, grip the heel, lancet flat, wipe the first drop, scoop, elevate and press, no bandage, document.'],terms:[['Capillary puncture','Collecting blood by puncturing the skin with a lancet.'],['Calcaneus','The heel bone, which must be avoided during heel punctures.'],['Microcollection container','A small tube for capillary blood, often called a bullet.']],mcqs:[q('What is the maximum depth for an infant heel puncture?','2.0 mm','5 mm','1 cm','0.5 mm','Avoid the calcaneus.'),q('Which heel areas are safe for infant punctures?','Medial or lateral plantar surface','The center back curve','The arch','The toes','Outer edges of the bottom of the heel.'),q('Why is the first drop of blood wiped away?','It contains tissue fluid and alcohol residue','It is too thick','It is arterial','It is not necessary','Improves accuracy.'),q('How long should the heel be warmed?','3–5 minutes','30 seconds','15 minutes','It should be chilled','Per Skill V.'),q('Why is no bandage applied to an infant?','Choking risk','Infants do not bleed','It is too expensive','Bandages hide results','Infants may put it in their mouths.'),q('In the capillary order of draw, which comes first among these?','EDTA','Serum','Heparin','Gray','After blood gases.'),q('Which finger is preferred for adult capillary puncture?','Middle or ring','Thumb','Index','Pinky','Avoid the thumb and pinky.'),q('How should the infant be positioned for a heel stick?','Face up with the foot lower than the torso','Face down','Sitting up','Standing','Gravity helps blood flow.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to perform an infant heel puncture following HOSA Skill V, including site selection, depth, collection technique and aftercare, and why each precaution matters.',rubric:[['Identification and preparation',['two identifiers','parent','consent','face up','foot lower','warm','3-5']],['Site and puncture',['medial','lateral','plantar','calcaneus','2 mm','alcohol','dry','flat']],['Collection',['first drop','wipe','scoop','order of draw','milk','squeeze','card']],['Aftercare',['elevate','pressure','no bandage','choking','label','document','thank']]],model:'I review the request, identify the infant with two identifiers, explain the procedure and obtain consent from the parents and verify requirements. After hand hygiene and gloves, I place the infant face up with the foot lower than the torso so gravity helps blood flow. I choose the medial or lateral plantar surface of the heel, avoiding bruised or swollen areas and old puncture sites, because the center and back of the heel lie over the calcaneus and a puncture there could cause bone infection. I warm the site for 3–5 minutes to increase blood flow, clean it with alcohol using friction and let it air-dry so alcohol does not hemolyze the sample. I grasp the heel with my index finger around the arch and thumb around the bottom, place the lancet flat against the skin and activate it, making a puncture no more than 2 mm deep, and immediately discard the lancet in the sharps container. I lower the heel, apply gentle pressure until a drop forms, wipe away the first drop because it contains tissue fluid, and scoop drops into the containers in order of draw without milking, which would hemolyze or dilute the sample. Then I elevate the heel and apply pressure with gauze until bleeding stops, check the site but apply no bandage because of the choking risk, label the specimen, thank the parents, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene and document the procedure.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_HEEL,CDC_NBS,WHO_BLOOD]};

export const H9={title:'Specimen handling, processing and special collections',hook:'A perfect draw can still produce a useless result if the tube sits in a warm car for three hours, an ammonia sample is not iced or a bilirubin tube is left under bright lights. Specimen collection and processing is 16% of the test, and timing is part of the job: a trough drawn an hour late can make a drug dose look wrong.',objectives:['Apply special handling for chilled, warm and light-sensitive specimens.','Collect timed specimens such as fasting, glucose tolerance, therapeutic drug monitoring and blood cultures.','Process specimens by clotting, centrifuging and aliquoting correctly.','Recognize specimen rejection criteria and transport requirements.'],sections:[
['Special handling',[
'Some analytes change after collection unless handled correctly. **Chill** specimens in an **ice-water slurry** (not solid ice) for **ammonia**, **lactic acid**, **blood gases** (if not tested immediately), **parathyroid hormone** and some other tests, because cellular metabolism continues and changes the results. Keep specimens at **body temperature (37 °C)** for **cold agglutinins** and **cryoglobulins** by wrapping them in a heel warmer or using a heat block, because these proteins precipitate when cold.',
'**Protect from light** specimens for **bilirubin**, **vitamin B₁₂**, **folate**, **beta-carotene** and **vitamin A** by wrapping the tube in **aluminum foil** or using an amber container, because light breaks these down. **Bilirubin** in newborns is especially important. Always check the laboratory’s test directory for handling rules before collection.',
"Glucose is a special case: blood cells keep using glucose after collection—about **5–7% per hour** at room temperature—so a glucose specimen in a serum tube must be separated promptly or collected in a **gray-top** tube with sodium fluoride. Potassium rises if serum is left on cells too long. These changes are why the laboratory sets time limits between collection and processing.",
sort('How should it be handled?',['Chill in ice slurry','Keep at body temperature','Protect from light'],[['Ammonia',0],['Lactic acid',0],['Cold agglutinins',1],['Cryoglobulins',1],['Bilirubin',2],['Vitamin B₁₂',2]])]],
['Timed specimens',[
'**Fasting** specimens (glucose, lipids) are collected after **8–12 hours** without food; water is usually allowed. The **oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT)** measures how the body handles a glucose drink: a **fasting** sample is drawn, the patient drinks a measured glucose solution within about 5 minutes, and samples are drawn at **set times** (for example, 1 and 2 hours, or more). The patient must **stay at the site**, not eat, smoke or chew gum and drink only water as allowed. **Label each tube with the exact time.**',
'**Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)** measures drug levels: a **trough** is drawn shortly **before** the next dose (lowest level), and a **peak** is drawn at the time of the **highest** level after a dose. **Diurnal variation** requires morning collection for cortisol and iron. **Postprandial** tests are drawn after a meal. Record the actual collection time and any deviations.',
match('Timed test terms',[['Fasting','No food for 8–12 hours'],['Trough','Lowest drug level, just before the next dose'],['Peak','Highest drug level after a dose'],['OGTT','Fasting sample, glucose drink, timed draws'],['Postprandial','After a meal'],['Diurnal variation','Changes by time of day, such as cortisol']])]],
['Blood cultures',[
'**Blood cultures** detect bacteria or fungi in the blood (bacteremia, sepsis). They are often ordered in **two sets** from **two separate sites** or times, each with an **aerobic** and an **anaerobic** bottle. Because skin bacteria can **contaminate** the culture and cause a false positive, the site is cleaned with a stronger antiseptic, such as **chlorhexidine** (often with alcohol) for about 30 seconds of friction, and allowed to dry. The **bottle tops** are cleaned with alcohol.',
'Blood cultures are drawn **first** in the order of draw. With a butterfly, the **aerobic bottle is filled first** (air in the tubing goes into it); with a syringe, the **anaerobic** bottle is often filled first. Fill bottles to the volume line—underfilling reduces sensitivity. Label each set with the site and time.',
order('Blood culture collection',['Identify the patient and check the order for two sets','Clean the site with chlorhexidine and friction; let dry','Clean bottle tops with alcohol','Collect into culture bottles first in the order of draw','Fill to the marked volume','Label with site and time'])]],
['Processing specimens',[
'**Serum** tubes (red, gold SST) must **clot completely**—usually **30–60 minutes** upright at room temperature—before centrifuging; centrifuging too early leaves fibrin strands. **Plasma** tubes (green, light blue) can be spun right away. Centrifuge with **stoppers on** to prevent aerosols, and **balance** the centrifuge with tubes of equal size and volume opposite each other. Never open a centrifuge until it has stopped completely.',
'**Aliquoting** means transferring serum or plasma into labeled **aliquot tubes** for different tests, using transfer pipettes, never pouring. Many tests require separating serum or plasma from cells within **2 hours** of collection. Label aliquots with the same information as the original tube plus the specimen type. Wear PPE and use a splash shield when removing stoppers.',
chk('Why must a centrifuge be balanced?',['An unbalanced load can damage the centrifuge and break tubes','It spins faster','It keeps samples cold','It is not necessary'],'Place equal tubes opposite each other.')]],
['Transport, rejection and point-of-care testing',[
'Specimens travel in **leak-proof biohazard bags** with requisitions in the outside pocket, upright to promote clotting and reduce agitation. Pneumatic tube systems speed transport but are not used for some specimens. Common **rejection criteria**: **unlabeled or mislabeled** tubes, **mismatched** requisitions, **hemolysis**, **clotted** anticoagulant tubes, **underfilled** light-blue tubes, wrong tube type, **expired** tubes, improper handling (not chilled) and specimens too old for testing.',
'Phlebotomists may perform **CLIA-waived point-of-care tests (POCT)** such as glucose, hemoglobin, hemoglobin A1c, coagulation (INR) and pregnancy tests. Run **quality control** as required, follow timing exactly and record results and QC. Report critical values immediately per policy.',
"Shipping specimens to reference laboratories adds rules: specimens are packed in **triple packaging** (a leak-proof primary container, a leak-proof secondary bag with absorbent material and a rigid outer box) and labeled under U.S. Department of Transportation and IATA rules as **UN3373 Biological Substance, Category B** for most diagnostic specimens. Frozen specimens travel on dry ice, which has its own hazard label. Temperature logs show the specimen stayed within the required range.",
sort('Accept or reject?',['Likely accepted','Likely rejected'],[['Properly labeled SST, clotted and spun',0],['Unlabeled lavender tube',1],['Half-filled light-blue tube',1],['Hemolyzed potassium sample',1],['Ammonia on ice delivered in 15 minutes',0],['Clotted lavender tube for CBC',1]])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Timed and special handling',{a:{text:'A requisition lists an ammonia level and a vancomycin trough due before the 10:00 dose. It is 9:40. What do you do?',choices:[['Collect at about 9:30–10:00 per policy before the dose, and place the ammonia tube in an ice slurry','b','Correct. Trough before the dose; ammonia chilled.',true],['Collect at noon when convenient','b','A late trough is not a trough.',false]]},b:{text:'A patient in the OGTT says she wants to step outside to smoke between draws. What do you say?',choices:[['Explain that smoking and leaving can affect results; she should remain and drink only water as allowed','c','Correct. Smoking alters glucose results.',true],['That is fine','c','It changes results.',false]]},c:{text:'You label each tube with the exact collection time and transport the iced ammonia immediately.',end:true}}),
{t:'callout',kind:'mistake',title:'Common processing errors',text:'Centrifuging serum tubes before they clot, leaving serum on cells too long, pouring instead of pipetting aliquots, forgetting to label aliquots, using solid ice instead of an ice slurry and letting light-sensitive tubes sit uncovered.'}]]
],takeaways:['Chill ammonia and lactic acid in an ice slurry; keep cold agglutinins at 37 °C; protect bilirubin and B₁₂ from light.','Fasting 8–12 hours; OGTT = fasting draw, glucose drink and timed draws; trough before the dose, peak at the highest level; blood cultures need chlorhexidine prep, two sets and full bottles, drawn first.','Let serum clot 30–60 minutes, balance the centrifuge, aliquot with pipettes, separate within about 2 hours; reject unlabeled, hemolyzed, clotted or underfilled specimens.'],terms:[['Aliquot','A portion of a specimen transferred into a separate labeled tube.'],['Trough level','The lowest drug level, drawn just before the next dose.'],['Ice slurry','A mix of ice and water used to chill specimens evenly.']],mcqs:[q('Which specimen must be chilled?','Ammonia','Bilirubin','Cold agglutinins','CBC','Use an ice slurry.'),q('Which specimen must be protected from light?','Bilirubin','Ammonia','Lactic acid','Potassium','Wrap in foil.'),q('Cold agglutinin specimens are kept at:','Body temperature (37 °C)','On ice','Frozen','In sunlight','They precipitate in the cold.'),q('When is a trough level drawn?','Just before the next dose','At the peak time','Any time','24 hours after the dose','Lowest drug level.'),q('How long should a serum tube clot before centrifuging?','About 30–60 minutes','5 minutes','6 hours','It should not clot','Complete clotting prevents fibrin.'),q('Which antiseptic is commonly used for blood culture site prep?','Chlorhexidine','Plain water','Soap only','Hydrogen peroxide','Reduces contamination.'),q('Which is a common rejection reason?','Unlabeled tube','Correct inversion','Proper chilling','Complete fill','Never test unlabeled specimens.'),q('How many hours of fasting are typical for fasting tests?','8–12','1–2','24–48','None','Water usually allowed.')],frq:{prompt:'A requisition lists a fasting glucose, an ammonia, a bilirubin and two sets of blood cultures for one patient. Explain the order of collection, the special handling for each specimen and how you would process and transport them.',rubric:[['Blood cultures',['first','chlorhexidine','two sets','aerobic','anaerobic','fill']],['Special handling',['ice slurry','ammonia','foil','light','bilirubin','gray','fasting']],['Processing',['clot','centrifuge','balance','aliquot','separate','2 hours']],['Transport and labeling',['label','time','biohazard','upright','immediately','reject']]],model:'I confirm the patient has fasted 8–12 hours. I collect the blood cultures first: I clean the site with chlorhexidine using friction and let it dry, clean the bottle tops with alcohol, fill the aerobic and anaerobic bottles to the marked volume and repeat from a second site or time for the second set, labeling each with the site and time. Then I collect the other tubes in the order of draw—for example, a gold SST for bilirubin, a green tube for ammonia and a gray tube for glucose—inverting each gently. I place the ammonia tube immediately in an ice slurry because cell metabolism would falsely raise ammonia, wrap the bilirubin tube in aluminum foil because light breaks bilirubin down and rely on the gray tube’s fluoride to stop glycolysis for glucose. I label all tubes at the bedside with the patient’s identifiers, date, time and my initials, then transport them upright in a leak-proof biohazard bag, with the iced ammonia delivered immediately. In processing, plasma tubes can be centrifuged right away, while the SST must clot for 30–60 minutes; I balance the centrifuge, spin with stoppers on, separate serum or plasma within about 2 hours and pipette labeled aliquots, knowing that unlabeled, hemolyzed or mishandled specimens would be rejected.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_CULTURE,MEDLINE_GTT,WHO_BLOOD]};

export const H10={title:'Non-blood specimens and 24-hour urine collection (Skill VIII)',hook:'Phlebotomists collect more than blood. They instruct patients on urine collections, help with throat swabs and stool kits and witness drug-screen collections where the chain of custody must hold up in court. A 24-hour urine test is only as good as the patient’s instructions: miss one void and the result is wrong. Skill VIII tests your teaching in 5 minutes.',objectives:['Describe urine specimen types, including random, first morning, clean-catch midstream and timed.','Instruct a patient on a 24-hour urine collection following HOSA Skill VIII.','Explain chain-of-custody procedures for drug testing.','Describe collection of throat, nasal, stool and other specimens.'],sections:[
['Types of urine specimens',[
'A **random** urine specimen can be collected at any time for routine screening. A **first morning** specimen is the most concentrated and is preferred for pregnancy tests and some screenings. A **clean-catch midstream** specimen is used for **cultures**: the patient cleans the genital area with the wipes provided, begins urinating into the toilet, collects the **middle portion** in a sterile container without touching the inside and finishes in the toilet. **Catheterized** and **suprapubic** specimens are collected by nurses or providers.',
'**Timed** specimens include the **24-hour urine**, used to measure substances excreted over a whole day—such as **creatinine clearance**, **protein**, **calcium**, **cortisol** and hormones. Urine should be tested within **2 hours** or refrigerated, because bacteria multiply and cells break down at room temperature.',
match('Urine specimen types',[['Random','Collected at any time'],['First morning','Most concentrated; pregnancy tests'],['Clean-catch midstream','Culture; middle of the stream'],['24-hour','Total excretion over a day'],['Catheterized','Collected through a catheter by a nurse'],['Within 2 hours','Test or refrigerate urine']])]],
['Starting the 24-hour collection: Skill VIII steps 1–8',[
'Skill VIII has 17 steps in 5 minutes, and most are **instructions** you give the patient. **1.** Review the test request. **2.** Approach, greet the patient and identify yourself. **3.** Identify the patient by asking them to **state their full name and date of birth and spell their names**. **4.** Explain the procedure and obtain consent. **5.** **Label the container** with the patient’s name and date of birth. **6.** Label it with the **specific preservative** and any **precautions** given in the scenario—some containers contain acid, which can burn skin.',
'**7.** Tell the patient **not to urinate directly into the container**, because the preservative could **splash**; urinate into a clean collection device (such as a toilet “hat” or cup) and pour it in. **8.** On the first morning, the patient **urinates upon awakening and discards** that urine—this empties the bladder so the 24-hour period starts with an empty bladder.',
order('Skill VIII start',['Review the test request','Greet the patient and identify yourself','Patient states and spells name and gives DOB','Explain and obtain consent','Label with name and DOB','Label with preservative and precautions','Do not urinate directly into the container','Void on awakening and discard that urine'])]],
['During and ending the collection: steps 9–17',[
'**9.** The patient **notes the date and time** of that first (discarded) void on the specimen label—this is the **start time**. **10.** **Collect all urine** for the next **24 hours**. **11.** **Refrigerate** the container throughout, if required by the scenario, or keep it in a cooler with ice (the rating sheet allows a chest placed in the bathtub). **12.** Urinate **before** a bowel movement, not after, to avoid contamination. **13.** **Drink a normal amount of fluid** unless directed otherwise.',
'**14.** On the **second morning**, urinate at the **same time** as the start, **add it to the container**, note the **end time** on the label and **seal** the container. **15.** **Inform staff** when the test is complete so it can be transported to the lab. **16–17.** Communicate appropriately and use standard precautions throughout. If the patient misses a void or spills urine, the collection must usually be **restarted**.',
flip('24-hour urine rules',[['Start','Void on awakening, discard, note the time'],['Collect','Every void for 24 hours'],['Container','Do not urinate directly into it'],['Storage','Refrigerate or keep on ice if required'],['Bowel movements','Urinate before, not after'],['End','Void at the same time next morning, add it, note the time, seal']])]],
['Drug testing and chain of custody',[
'**Forensic** and workplace drug tests follow **chain-of-custody** procedures so results are legally defensible. The collector verifies the donor’s **photo ID**, has the donor empty pockets and wash hands, adds **bluing agent** to the toilet water and turns off running water to prevent dilution or substitution. The specimen’s **temperature** is checked within **4 minutes** (an acceptable range is about **32–38 °C / 90–100 °F**). The specimen is sealed with tamper-evident tape, initialed by the donor and documented on a **custody and control form** every time it changes hands.',
'Other legal specimens include **blood alcohol** tests, which require cleaning the site with a **non-alcohol antiseptic** (such as povidone-iodine or soap and water) so alcohol does not contaminate the result, and specimens for **paternity** or **DNA** testing. Any break in the chain of custody can make a result inadmissible.',
order('Chain of custody basics',['Verify photo ID','Donor empties pockets and washes hands','Add bluing agent; turn off water','Check temperature within 4 minutes','Seal with tamper-evident tape; donor initials','Complete the custody and control form'])]],
['Other non-blood specimens',[
'**Throat swabs** for strep tests are collected by having the patient tilt the head back and open wide, depressing the tongue and swabbing both **tonsillar areas** and the **back of the throat** without touching the tongue, cheeks or teeth. **Nasopharyngeal swabs** for influenza or COVID-19 pass gently through the nostril to the back of the nasal cavity. **Stool** specimens test for occult blood, parasites or pathogens and are collected in clean containers without urine or toilet water. **Semen**, **sputum** (deep cough, first morning) and **sweat chloride** tests (for cystic fibrosis) have their own instructions.',
'For every non-blood specimen, give clear instructions, label the container (not the lid) with the patient’s identifiers, date and time, note the specimen source and deliver it promptly or store it as directed. Wear gloves and treat all specimens as potentially infectious.',
"Some specimens are collected for **point-of-care** testing at the collection site, such as **rapid strep** and **urine pregnancy** tests. Follow the kit instructions exactly, read results only at the specified time, confirm the internal **control line** appears and record the result with the kit’s lot number. A test read too early or too late can give a false result.",
sort('Specimen and instruction',['Throat swab','Clean-catch urine','Stool','Sputum'],[['Swab both tonsillar areas without touching the tongue',0],['Collect the middle of the stream after cleaning',1],['Keep free of urine and toilet water',2],['Deep cough on waking, not saliva',3],['Avoid touching the inside of the sterile cup',1],['Depress the tongue',0]])]],
['Quality and practice',[
'Most non-blood specimen errors come from poor instructions. Use plain language, check understanding with **teach-back** (“Tell me how you will start your collection tomorrow”), provide written instructions and ask about barriers such as work schedules or lack of a refrigerator. Document the instructions given and the times reported.',
kase('24-hour urine teaching',{a:{text:'A patient asks, “Should I save my first urine tomorrow morning?” What do you tell him?',choices:[['No—urinate on awakening, discard it, and write that time as the start','b','Correct. The first void is discarded to start with an empty bladder.',true],['Yes, save every drop including the first','b','The first morning void is discarded on day one.',false]]},b:{text:'He asks if he can urinate directly into the container to save time. What do you say?',choices:[['No—the preservative may splash; use the collection cup and pour it in','c','Correct. Some preservatives are caustic.',true],['Yes, that is fine','c','Splashing preservative is a safety risk.',false]]},c:{text:'He repeats the instructions back correctly, including the end time and refrigeration. Teaching complete.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Urine types: random, first morning, clean-catch midstream (cultures) and timed; test within 2 hours or refrigerate.','Skill VIII: identify, label with name, DOB, preservative and precautions; do not urinate directly into the container; discard the first void and note the time; collect all urine 24 hours; refrigerate; urinate before bowel movements; normal fluids; end with a void at the same time, note and seal; tell staff.','Chain of custody: photo ID, bluing agent, temperature within 4 minutes, tamper-evident seal and custody form; use non-alcohol antiseptic for blood alcohol.'],terms:[['Clean-catch midstream','A urine sample collected from the middle of the stream after cleansing.'],['Chain of custody','Documentation of every person who handles a legal specimen.'],['Preservative','A chemical added to a container to keep a specimen stable.']],mcqs:[q('What does the patient do with the first morning void when starting a 24-hour urine?','Discard it and note the time','Save it in the container','Freeze it','Take it to the lab','The bladder must start empty.'),q('Why shouldn’t the patient urinate directly into the 24-hour container?','The preservative may splash','It is too small','It must stay sterile on the outside','It makes the urine too warm','Some preservatives are acids.'),q('How should the 24-hour collection end?','Void at the same time the next morning, add it, note the time and seal','Stop whenever convenient','Discard the last void','After 12 hours','Completes 24 hours.'),q('What should the patient do before a bowel movement during the collection?','Urinate first','Nothing special','Stop the collection','Drink extra water','Prevents contamination.'),q('Which urine specimen is used for cultures?','Clean-catch midstream','Random','24-hour','First morning without cleansing','Reduces contamination.'),q('Within what time should a drug-screen urine temperature be checked?','4 minutes','1 hour','24 hours','It is not checked','Detects substitution.'),q('Which antiseptic is used for a blood alcohol draw?','A non-alcohol antiseptic such as povidone-iodine','70% isopropyl alcohol','Hand sanitizer','No antiseptic','Avoid alcohol contamination.'),q('For a throat swab, you should avoid touching:','The tongue, cheeks and teeth','The tonsils','The back of the throat','The swab packaging','Contamination with mouth flora.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would instruct a patient to collect a 24-hour urine specimen following HOSA Skill VIII, including labeling, safety and storage, and explain why each instruction matters.',rubric:[['Identification and labeling',['state','spell','date of birth','label','preservative','precautions']],['Starting',['awakening','discard','note the time','start','empty bladder']],['During collection',['all urine','24 hours','refrigerate','splash','bowel movement','normal fluid']],['Ending and follow-up',['same time','add','seal','end time','inform staff','teach-back','restart']]],model:'I review the request, greet the patient, introduce myself and identify them by having them state and spell their full name and give their date of birth. I explain the test and obtain consent, then label the container with the patient’s name and date of birth and with the specific preservative and any precautions, because some preservatives are caustic. I tell the patient not to urinate directly into the container, since the preservative could splash, but to use a collection cup and pour the urine in. On the first morning they should urinate on awakening and discard that urine, so the collection starts with an empty bladder, and write that date and time on the label as the start. For the next 24 hours they collect every void, because missing one makes the total wrong and usually requires restarting, and they keep the container refrigerated or in a cooler on ice if required to prevent bacterial growth. They should urinate before a bowel movement to avoid contamination and drink a normal amount of fluid unless told otherwise. On the second morning, at the same time as the start, they urinate one last time, add it to the container, note the end time and seal the container, then tell the staff so it can be transported. I check understanding with teach-back and communicate clearly throughout.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_URINE24,MEDLINE_CLEAN,SAMHSA_DT]};
